\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Unless a section says otherwise, ${\mathbb{N}}=\{1,2,\ldots\}$,
${\mathbb{Z}}$ is the ring of integers, ${\mathbb{Q}},{\mathbb{R}},{\mathbb{C}}$ are the rational, real, and complex
fields, $[N]=\{1,\ldots,\lfloor N\rfloor\}$, and $\log$ is the natural
logarithm. For real functions of a parameter tending to infinity,
$f\ll g$ and $f=O(g)$ mean $|f|\leq Cg$ eventually for some fixed $C$;
$f\gg g$ reverses this comparison; $f=o(g)$ means $f/g\to0$;
$f\sim g$ means $f/g\to1$; and $f\asymp g$ means both order bounds.
A subscript on $O$ or $\ll$ specifies allowed constant dependence.
The expression $n^{o(1)}$ in an upper bound means growth smaller than
$n^\epsilon$ for each fixed $\epsilon>0$. Local definitions govern any
exception, finite-field convention, or use of the same symbol for another
object. An asymptotic claim is not automatically an effective algorithm.



Use one infinite independent sign sequence; the degree-$n$ polynomial is its prefix. Thus the almost-sure assertion is on one probability space and is stronger than a statement only in probability. Real zeros are counted with multiplicity under the conventional root-count formulation. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]} \href{https://www.erdosproblems.com/521}{[S2]}

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $(\epsilon_k)_{k\geq 0}$ be independently uniformly chosen at random from $\{-1,1\}$. If $R_n$ counts the number of real roots of $f_n(z)=\sum_{0\leq k\leq n}\epsilon_k z^k$ then is it true that, almost surely, $ \lim_{n\to \infty}\frac{R_n}{\log n}=\frac{2}{\pi}? $ \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\paragraph{Scope and interpretation.}

The root-multiplicity convention is recorded explicitly. The archived statement does not separately formulate a distinct-real-zero variant. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\paragraph{Residual task reported by the archive.}

The embedded review (2026-08-17) records: Control the roots outside [-1,1] for the same infinite coefficient sequence, or independently verify and publish the claimed counterargument. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

{\small\emph{Transcription note.} Only unambiguous escape/formatting damage and nonmathematical serialization debris were repaired in the displayed source statement.}

\subsection{Short English statement}
For one infinite sequence of independent random signs, do the real-root counts of its initial polynomials almost surely have the predicted logarithmic limit?

